# Summary of Title V — Committee on Energy and Natural Resources

## Subtitle A — Oil and Gas Leasing

**Sec. 50101. Onshore oil and gas leasing.**
This section repeals two Inflation Reduction Act (IRA) provisions: the higher onshore royalty rate (restoring the prior rate) and restrictions on noncompetitive leasing. It requires the Secretary of the Interior to immediately resume quarterly onshore oil and gas lease sales, mandating a minimum of 4 sales per fiscal year in each of nine states: Wyoming, New Mexico, Colorado, Utah, Montana, North Dakota, Oklahoma, Nevada, and Alaska. Each sale must offer at least 50% of nominated parcels, and replacement sales are required if a sale is canceled or if 25% or more of offered acreage goes unbid. It also amends the Mineral Leasing Act to require parcels be made available for leasing within 18 months of an expression of interest, sets a non-renewable 4-year term for drilling permits, and eases rules for commingling production from multiple sources.

**Sec. 50102. Offshore oil and gas leasing.**
The Secretary of the Interior must conduct at least 30 region-wide oil and gas lease sales in the Gulf of Mexico ("Gulf of America") region between 2025 and 2040 (at least 1 by December 15, 2025; 2 per year in 2026–2039; 1 in 2040), plus at least 6 lease sales in Alaska's Cook Inlet Planning Area (1 each in 2026–2028 and 2030–2032). Gulf sales must offer at least 80,000,000 acres each with a royalty rate between 12.5% and 16.67%, while Cook Inlet sales must offer at least 1,000,000 acres each; beginning in FY2034, Cook Inlet revenues are split 70% to Alaska and 30% to the Treasury. The section repeals the IRA's offshore royalty rate increase and caps offshore royalties at 16.67% in the Outer Continental Shelf Lands Act. It also limits Gulf of Mexico Energy Security Act revenue-sharing to $650 million annually for FY2025–2034 and $500 million annually for FY2035–2055, and directs the Secretary to approve offshore commingling requests unless unsafe or recovery-reducing.

**Sec. 50103. Royalties on extracted methane.**
This section repeals Section 50263 of the Inflation Reduction Act, which had imposed fees/royalties on methane waste from oil and gas production on federal lands. The repeal eliminates the IRA's methane emissions charge program administered under 30 U.S.C. 1727.

**Sec. 50104. Alaska oil and gas leasing.**
The Secretary of the Interior (through the Bureau of Land Management) must conduct at least 4 area-wide lease sales in the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge Coastal Plain within 10 years of enactment, offering at least 400,000 acres per sale with the first sale within 1 year, the second within 3 years, the third within 5 years, and the fourth within 7 years. Sales must use the terms from the August 2020 Coastal Plain Oil and Gas Leasing Program Record of Decision and target areas with the highest hydrocarbon potential. Revenue sharing is set at 50% to the State of Alaska for FY2025–2033, rising to 70% for FY2034 and thereafter, with the remainder deposited in the Treasury as miscellaneous receipts.

**Sec. 50105. National Petroleum Reserve-Alaska.**
The Secretary of the Interior must restore and resume the oil and gas leasing program in the National Petroleum Reserve-Alaska based on the June 2020 BLM Integrated Activity Plan Final Environmental Impact Statement and December 2020 Record of Decision (excluding the September 2022 errata). At least 5 lease sales must be held within 10 years of enactment, each offering at least 4,000,000 acres, with the first within 1 year and subsequent sales at least every 2 years. Beginning in FY2034, receipts from these leases are split 70% to the State of Alaska and 30% to the U.S. Treasury.

## Subtitle B — Mining

**Sec. 50201. Coal leasing.**
Within 90 days of enactment, the Secretary of the Interior must process all pending coal lease applications (and those submitted within 90 days) under the Bureau of Land Management's lease-by-application program by publishing environmental reviews, establishing fair market value, holding lease sales, and identifying winning bidders. The Secretary may also grant additional approvals needed for mining to begin on previously issued coal leases.

**Sec. 50202. Coal royalty.**
The federal coal royalty rate is reduced from 12.5% to no more than 7% for the period from enactment through September 30, 2034, under Section 7(a) of the Mineral Leasing Act. This reduced rate applies to all coal leases issued before, on, or after enactment that have not been terminated. Lessees who paid advance royalties before enactment receive a credit for the difference between what they paid and what they would have owed at the lower rate.

**Sec. 50203. Leases for known recoverable coal resources.**
Within 90 days of enactment, the Secretary of the Interior must make at least 4,000,000 additional acres of known recoverable coal resources on federal land available for leasing across the 48 contiguous states and Alaska. The mandate overrides certain leasing restrictions but excludes land within National Monuments, National Recreation Areas, Wilderness Preservation System components, Wild and Scenic Rivers, National Trails, National Conservation Areas, Wildlife Refuges, Fish Hatcheries, and National Park System units.

**Sec. 50204. Authorization to mine Federal coal.**
Federal coal reserves on land with mining plans already approved by the Secretary of the Interior as of enactment—and adjacent to state or private coal reserves that couldn't otherwise be mined economically—are authorized to be mined. Within 90 days of enactment, the Secretary must take steps to authorize this mining without substantial modification, though NEPA review is still permitted.

## Subtitle C — Lands

**Sec. 50301. Timber sales and long-term contracting for the Forest Service and the Bureau of Land Management.**
For FY2026–2034, the Forest Service must annually sell at least 250,000,000 more board-feet of timber than the prior year on National Forest System land, and the BLM must sell at least 20,000,000 more board-feet annually on public lands, both subject to existing plans. From FY2025–2034, the Forest Service must enter at least 40 long-term timber sale contracts and the BLM at least 5 long-term vegetative materials contracts, each with terms of at least 20 years plus extension options. All receipts from these contracts go to the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 50302. Renewable energy fees on Federal land.**
The Secretaries of the Interior and Agriculture must collect annual acreage rents from wind and solar right-of-way holders on public and National Forest System lands, calculated as the 5-year average per-acre pastureland rental rate (from the USDA Cash Rents Survey) multiplied by an encumbrance factor (100% for solar; at least 10% for wind) with a 3% annual adjustment. Holders must also pay an annual capacity fee equal to the greater of the acreage rent or 3.9% of gross electricity proceeds, though wind projects may request a 10% multiple-use reduction if at least 25% of the right-of-way area is used for non-wind purposes. Late payments incur fees after 15 days, and rights-of-way may be terminated if payment is not received within 90 days.

**Sec. 50303. Renewable energy revenue sharing.**
Beginning January 1, 2026, revenues from wind and solar projects on covered federal land (bonus bids, rentals, fees, and other payments) are deposited in the Treasury and allocated with 25% paid to the host state and 25% distributed among affected counties based on the share of county land generating the revenue. These payments follow the usage rules of Section 35 of the Mineral Leasing Act and are in addition to payments in lieu of taxes under chapter 69 of title 31, U.S. Code.

**Sec. 50304. Rescission of National Park Service and Bureau of Land Management funds.**
This section rescinds the unobligated balances of three Inflation Reduction Act sections: 50221, 50222, and 50223 (all at 136 Stat. 2052), which had funded National Park Service and Bureau of Land Management conservation, land acquisition, and deferred maintenance programs.

**Sec. 50305. Celebrating America's 250th anniversary.**
This section appropriates $150,000,000 to the Secretary of the Interior (through the Director of the National Park Service) for FY2025 to fund events, celebrations, and activities commemorating the 250th anniversary of the founding of the United States. The funds remain available through fiscal year 2028.

## Subtitle D — Energy

**Sec. 50401. Strategic Petroleum Reserve.**
This section appropriates $218,000,000 to the Department of Energy for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) for maintenance and repairs of Strategic Petroleum Reserve storage and related facilities, plus $171,000,000 to purchase petroleum products for the Reserve. It also repeals Section 20003 of Public Law 115-97, which had mandated drawdowns and sales from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve.

**Sec. 50402. Repeals; rescissions.**
Section 50142 of the Inflation Reduction Act is repealed and its unobligated balance rescinded. Additionally, unobligated balances are rescinded from seven other IRA sections: 50123, 50141, 50144, 50145, 50151, 50152, 50153, and 50161—covering programs such as energy infrastructure financing, DOE loan programs, and energy efficiency grants.

**Sec. 50403. Energy dominance financing.**
Section 1706 of the Energy Policy Act of 2005 is amended to expand eligible activities to include projects that increase capacity or output and support electric supply for grid reliability, and to cover the full energy and critical minerals supply chain (identification through generation). It appropriates $1,000,000,000 to the Secretary for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2028) for these activities, with no more than 3% for administrative costs. The commitment authority expiration in IRA Section 50144(b) is extended from 2026 to 2028.

**Sec. 50404. Transformational artificial intelligence models.**
The Secretary of Energy must mobilize National Laboratories to partner with U.S. industry to curate Department of Energy scientific data for use in artificial intelligence and machine learning models, and to initiate seed efforts for self-improving AI models for science and engineering. The curated data may support next-generation microelectronics development, and the AI models will be shared with the scientific community via the "American science cloud" to accelerate energy technology innovation. This section is funded with an appropriation of $150,000,000, available through September 30, 2026.

## Subtitle E — Water

**Sec. 50501. Water conveyance and surface water storage enhancement.**
This section appropriates $1,000,000,000 to the Secretary of the Interior (through the Commissioner of Reclamation) for FY2025, available through September 30, 2034, for construction activities that restore or increase the capacity or use of existing Bureau of Reclamation conveyance facilities or increase the capacity of existing Reclamation surface water storage facilities. Contracts entered into under this section are not treated as new or amended contracts for purposes of the Reclamation Reform Act of 1982 and the Reclamation Projects Authorization and Adjustment Act of 1992. None of the funds are reimbursable or subject to matching or cost-sharing requirements. [end of text]


